## <div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0; font-size: 2em;">Lesson Planner – Theme # It's Sunny! – Lesson # 2</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.2em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Lesson #:</b> 2</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Skills Focus:</b> Reading</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Grade:</b> 1st Grade (CEFR: Pre-A1.3)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Scenario:</b> Nice Weather Today!</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Theme:</b> It's Sunny!</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Specific Objective:</b> Reading: Identify one-step written directions with visual support. Reading: Match simple words to corresponding pictures. Phonemic Awareness: Identify sounds of the letters of the alphabet when supported with a concrete item or picture representing that sound.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Learning Outcome:</b> Reading: Can identify weather-related words in simple text instructions (e.g., matching "sun" to a picture of the sun). Can match simple weather-related words to corresponding pictures (e.g., matching the word "sunny" to a picture of a sunny day). Can identify sounds of the letters of the alphabet when supported with a concrete item or picture representing that sound in familiar words related to weather (e.g., identifying the sound of the letter /r/ in the word "rainy" when shown a picture of raindrops).</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification. Activate prior knowledge about weather and introduce target vocabulary for reading.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher greets students with a "Hello Song" incorporating simple weather actions (e.g., mimicking sun shining, rain falling, wind blowing). The teacher asks, "What is the weather today?" while pointing outside or to a large weather chart. Students respond chorally "It is sunny!" or "It is rainy!" using familiar vocabulary from Lesson 1. The teacher shows large flashcards for 'sun', 'rain', 'cloud', 'wind' and associated adjectives 'sunny', 'rainy', 'cloudy', 'windy'.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher holds up a flashcard, for example, "sun," and says the word clearly, then points to the written word "sun" on the card. The teacher models the pronunciation of /s/ in "sunny" and /r/ in "raining" by exaggerating the sounds. For 'sunny', the teacher says "Sss-unny" and points to the sun picture. For 'rainy', "Rrr-ainy" and points to the rain picture. The teacher then shows a simple written direction, "Draw a sun," and models drawing a sun on the board while reading the words aloud slowly.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Showing a picture of rain) "Is this sunny or rainy?" (Expected answer: "Rainy!")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Pointing to the written word "sun" next to a picture of the sun) "What word is this?" (Expected answer: "Sun!")</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Nice Weather Today!". Students are exposed to the target reading content.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A large, colorful weather chart with simple illustrated sentences describing weather conditions (e.g., "It is sunny. Look at the sun!", "It is raining. See the rain!"). Individual student worksheets with large pictures and corresponding weather words or short, one-step instructions.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Reading: Identify weather-related words in simple text instructions and match simple words to corresponding pictures.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher shows the large weather chart. The teacher reads aloud a simple sentence like "It is sunny." and students point to the picture of the sun. The teacher then reads "It is raining." and students point to the rain picture.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students receive a worksheet with three weather pictures (sun, rain, cloud) and three corresponding words below (e.g., "sun", "rain", "cloud"). The teacher reads each word aloud, and students point to the matching word and picture.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher gives a simple direction: "Circle the picture of the sun." or "Draw a line to the word 'rainy'." Students follow these one-step written directions with visual support.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Reading. Students practice identifying and matching weather words and following simple instructions.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students will practice reading simple weather words (sunny, rainy, cloudy, windy, sun, rain, clouds, wind) and matching them to pictures. They will also practice identifying the initial sounds of /s/ in "sunny" and /r/ in "rainy," and /w/ in "weather." Grammar focus on "It is sunny/raining/cloudy."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 1 (Controlled Practice - Matching):</b> Students receive a worksheet with two columns. Column A has pictures of weather (sun, rain, cloud, wind). Column B has the corresponding words (sun, rain, cloud, wind). Students draw a line from each picture to its matching word. The teacher circulates, provides individual help, and checks for correct matching.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 2 (Semi-controlled - Circle the Word):</b> The teacher displays a large picture of "It is sunny." On the students' individual worksheets, they see the same picture with two word options below it, e.g., "sunny" and "rainy". Students circle the correct word that matches the picture. Repeat with other weather conditions (e.g., "It is rainy," "It is cloudy," "It is windy"). The teacher verbally reinforces the initial sounds /s/, /r/, /w/ while referring to the words.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 3 (One-step Directions with Peer Check):</b> Students are given a worksheet with an empty box and a written instruction, e.g., "Draw a sun." or "Color the rain blue." Students read the instruction and follow it. After completing, they turn to a partner and show their drawing/coloring, saying "I drew a sun!" or "This is rain." Their partner checks if the drawing matches the written instruction. The teacher monitors for correct reading and actions.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Nice Weather Today!".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> "Our Class Weather Report" - a collaborative reading and matching activity to create a visual weather display.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher divides the class into small groups (3-4 students). Each group receives a large poster board and a set of weather-related picture cut-outs (sun, rain, clouds, wind, snow) and word strips (sunny, rainy, cloudy, windy, snowy). They also get strips with simple sentences like "It is sunny." or "It is raining."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Each group's task is to read the word strips and sentence strips, then match them to the correct picture cut-outs. For example, they read "It is sunny." and paste it next to the sun picture. They then paste the word "sunny" below the picture.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher asks groups to read the initial letter sounds of /s/ in "sunny" or /r/ in "rainy" as they place the words.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">When finished, each group presents their "Weather Report" poster. One student from each group points to a picture and attempts to read the corresponding word or sentence (e.g., pointing to the sun picture and reading "Sunny!" or "It is sunny."). The teacher facilitates and encourages.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy to check understanding and application of reading skills.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Observation Checklist (teacher notes student ability to match words/pictures and follow instructions) and an individual exit ticket.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students receive an "Exit Ticket" worksheet. It contains three sections:
                    <ol style="list-style-type: decimal; padding-left: 20px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Section 1: Three simple weather pictures (sun, rain, cloud) and three words to match. Students draw lines to match each picture to its word.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Section 2: A simple instruction like "Draw the wind." Students read and draw the correct weather.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Section 3: A picture of a sunny day with two words below it: "sunny" and "cold". Students circle the correct adjective.</li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Can identify (match) simple weather words to corresponding pictures.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Can follow one-step written directions with visual support (e.g., drawing based on a word).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Can identify the initial sounds of /s/ in "sunny" and /r/ in "rainy" when presented with the words and pictures.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> On the back of the worksheet, students circle their answer for:
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I can read weather words like 'sun' and 'rain'." (Yes / No / A little)</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I can draw what the words say." (Yes / No / A little)</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Review of the lesson and planning for future learning.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was your favorite weather word to read today?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was easy or hard about matching the words to pictures?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> The students showed good engagement with the picture matching activities, and most were able to follow one-step written directions with strong visual support. The phonemic awareness focus on /s/ and /r/ was successful, though some students still need more practice differentiating initial sounds. The collaborative poster activity helped reinforce reading in a fun context. For the next lesson, more speaking practice will be needed, building on the words they can now read. I will need to provide more sentence frames for verbal descriptions.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> In Lesson 3 (Speaking & Interaction), students will use the weather words they learned and read today to actively speak and interact in pairs, describing simple weather conditions and asking/answering questions like "What is the weather today?" based on visual prompts.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>